TPUSA Deletes Chris D’Elia From the Tour Lineup After His Profane Instagram Story, Then Faces the Same Transparency Questions It Asks of Others

Baron Coleman reviews a listener tip that Turning Point USA quietly removed comedian Chris D’Elia from an announced campus event. The Instagram post that had listed him is gone, the archived tour lineup from mid-September no longer matches the current one, and D’Elia posted a story telling people to shut up and declaring he was not doing the TPUSA appearance. Coleman notes D’Elia’s prior allegations and the tone of the reaction, then contrasts it with the response a public-relations adviser might have recommended: a short video taking responsibility, expressing disappointment for the students, and leaving the door open. Instead the organization simply deleted the announcement.

The segment then turns to an interview Blake Neff gave to Jeremy Boring. Neff describes TPUSA forwarding a TikTok to the FBI after the assassination of Charlie Kirk; the clip used ominous music and suggested something was going to happen at Utah Valley University. Neff says the organization also passed along other social-media material and expresses the wish that the FBI would release a fuller public account of what it found, even if the result is only that the posters had no advance knowledge. Coleman treats the request for transparency as reasonable, then applies the same standard to TPUSA itself. He asks where Neff and Danny Phillips were that morning, why a second rental car was needed, and why the organization has not accepted his standing offer of $10,000 toward the statue reward in exchange for the six-camera CCTV footage from the venue. The information that has surfaced, he notes, has arrived through outside podcasts rather than official channels.

The practical observation is that an organization which presents itself as the guardian of campus discourse is now managing the fallout from a booking decision while simultaneously arguing that more information about the Kirk case would be helpful. Whether the deletion of D’Elia was a response to public pressure or an internal decision, the episode leaves the same question that has followed the organization for more than a year: why the footage and the contemporaneous accounts remain unavailable to the same public that is asked to accept the official narrative.

